  "worked_solution": "Let $F$ be the event that a randomly selected person speaks a foreign language, and let $B$ be the event that a randomly selected person has a bachelor's degree. Then, we require $P(B|F),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=\\frac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(F|B)=0.9,$ since $90%$ of all people who have a bachelor's degree speak a foreign language.\n\n- $P(B)=0.31,$ since $31%$ of the adult population has a bachelor's degree.\n\n- $P(F)=0.63,$ since $63%$ of the adult population speak a foreign language.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|F) & =\\frac{P(F|B)P(B)}{P(F)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.9)(0.31)}{0.63} \\\\  & ≈44%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent.",
